{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FINDINGS (2026-07-24, from closing 620b / 8559 / 86a5)\n\nThe gap is NARROWER than this ticket states — one of the three subclasses already has a\nworking, legitimate close path — but there is also a SECOND stranding gate the ticket does\nnot mention.\n\n1. `--ref <commit>` IS a legitimate close path for the \"ACs invalidated by SUBSEQUENT work\"\n   subclass, and it works today. Demonstrated on 86a5-9221-0037-4e38: its ACs grep\n   `docs/passing-code-review.md`, which a LATER commit (c2bfbe217, \"package author guides\n   in-package\") reduced to a 453-byte pointer stub. Closing at worktree HEAD would have been\n   judged against a tree where the criteria are unsatisfiable. Closing with\n   `--ref f90850861` (the commit the work actually landed in) verified against the tree the\n   ACs were written for and PASSED — signed, no --force-close, no AC edit. This is the\n   correct escape hatch for this subclass and it should be documented as such in the close\n   workflow, not reinvented.\n\n   Note the flag already exists on BOTH surfaces: `rebar transition ... closed --ref <c>`\n   (`_commands/transition.py:165-171`, both `--ref X` and `--ref=X`) and library\n   `transition(..., ref=...)`, and it is documented at `docs/cli-reference.md:393`.\n\n2. `--ref` does NOT solve the other two subclasses this ticket names:\n   (a) ACs invalidated BY THE FIX ITSELF — there is no ref at which both the fix is present\n       and the original criteria are satisfiable; the two are mutually exclusive by\n       construction. This is the genuinely unaddressed case.\n   (b) AUTO-GENERATED / UNVERIFIABLE ACs — bad at every ref, so no ref selection helps.\n   (c) AUTOMATION-OWNED alert tickets (the originating f436-fac8-382f-47ec case) — closure is\n       owned by the filing automation, not by a code-verifiable criterion.\n\n3. NEW — a SECOND gate strands finished work, and it is expensive. Closing 86a5 first failed\n   on the PLAN-REVIEW close gate, not the completion verifier:\n\n       plan-review close gate: stale-regver: the criteria registry changed since the plan\n       review (overlay activated/edited/disabled). Health: legacy-unpinned (enforced)\n\n   The ticket's plan review was signed under rebar 0.8.0; the criteria registry has since\n   changed, so a ticket whose CODE WORK IS DONE AND MERGED could not close until a full\n   billable `rebar review-plan --force` re-review of its (already-executed) plan was run.\n   Re-reviewing a plan that has already shipped, purely to satisfy a registry-version check,\n   is the same class of defect as this ticket: a gate with no legitimate path for a ticket\n   whose work is genuinely complete. Whatever disposition contract comes out of this ticket\n   should cover the plan-review close gate too, not just the completion verifier.\n\n4. This ticket has NO Acceptance Criteria section of its own. That must be fixed before it\n   can be worked — as written, the completion verifier would auto-generate criteria for it,\n   which is precisely the failure mode the ticket exists to fix.\n\nRECOMMENDED SCOPE after these findings:\n- Document `--ref <landing-commit>` as the sanctioned close path for the invalidated-by-later-\n  work subclass (cheapest win; the mechanism already ships).\n- Design the auditable criterion-disposition record (moot / invalidated-by-fix / superseded)\n  bound to cited evidence + authorized provenance, for subclass (a).\n- Add an automation-owned closure branch for bot-filed alert tickets, for subclass (c).\n- Extend whatever disposition contract results to the plan-review close gate's stale-regver\n  path, so completed work is not forced through a re-review of an already-executed plan.\n\nMoving back to OPEN: the ticket was `in_progress` with no assignee and no live claim session\n(a stale claim), and it now needs a plan + ACs before any implementation.\n"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1784936648364649001,"uuid":"5a096b4d-808f-4bc3-84b7-1d8f2ac2df13"}